# Using the grid system

Use the native **Sections** view for a new dashboard. It sets the column widths and gaps for you, so cards line up at every screen size.

## Grid and columns

| Part | Size | Setting |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Section column | At least 320px and at most 500px wide | Set by Home Assistant |
| Gap between sections | 32px across and 24px down. 8px across on screens up to 600px wide | Set by Home Assistant |
| Sections side by side | Up to 4 by default | **Max sections side by side** in the view settings (`max_columns`) |
| Section width | 1 column, 2, 3 or full width | **Width** in the section settings (`column_span`) |
| Grid inside a section | 12 columns, with 56px rows and 8px gaps | Set by Home Assistant |

### Card widths

A card's width is a number of the section's 12 columns. Most cards choose a sensible size themselves. Change it only when the layout needs it.

| Card width | Columns | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Full | 12 | A heading, a graph, a thermostat or a map |
| Half | 6 | A Tile card, which is 6 columns by default |
| Third | 4 | 3 short readings in a row, such as room temperatures |
| Quarter | 3 | A button that has a short, clear name |

<%= colour_example('layout-section-grid', callout: false) %>

This YAML shows a Heating section for an invented household. The heading and the thermostat take the full width, and 2 Tile cards share a row.

```yaml
type: grid
cards:
  - type: heading
    heading: Heating
  - type: thermostat
    entity: climate.hallway
  - type: tile
    entity: sensor.kitchen_temperature
    name: Kitchen
    grid_options:
      columns: 6
  - type: tile
    entity: sensor.bedroom_temperature
    name: Bedroom
    grid_options:
      columns: 6
```

[Width overrides](structure-width-overrides.html) explains how to make a section or a card wider or narrower.
